{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Snowmelt Pulse: Nitrogen Fixers of a Caribbean Blue Hole",
    "date": "2026-10-08",
    "lesson": "The Great Blue Hole off Belize is a flooded karst sinkhole: a shallow rim of warm oxygenated water sits atop a stratified, anoxic deep chamber where hydrogen sulfide accumulates below ~30m. At the thin boundary layer, filamentous cyanobacteria fix atmospheric nitrogen using the nitrogenase enzyme, which is poisoned by oxygen - so they must balance photosynthesis (needs light, makes oxygen) against nitrogen fixation (needs darkness-like conditions, hates oxygen). This scenario asks you to live right at that seam: too shallow and your own photosynthetic oxygen shuts down fixation; too deep and the sulfide and dark kill you outright. Stalactite-draped limestone walls ring the hole, riddled with solution pockets where cells can shelter from grazers drifting up from the anoxic depths.",
    "citation": "Educational synthesis inspired by Belize Great Blue Hole stratification studies (Gischler et al.) and general diazotrophic cyanobacteria (Trichodesmium, Nostoc) physiology.",
    "difficulty": "hard",
    "realWorldBasis": "Great Blue Hole, Belize - karst sinkhole chemocline, anoxic/sulfidic basin beneath an oxygenated reef lagoon"
  },
